DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The aaddddrr22lliinnee utility translates program addresses specified by the com‐
     mand line arguments _h_e_x_a_d_d_r_e_s_s to their corresponding source file names
     and line numbers.  If no arguments are given to aaddddrr22lliinnee, it will read
     these addresses from standard input.

     Program addresses specified by arguments _h_e_x_a_d_d_r_e_s_s are encoded using the
     conventions accepted by strtoull(3).

     By default, aaddddrr22lliinnee will use the executable “_a_._o_u_t”.  The --ee option may
     be used to specified a different ELF object.

     The aaddddrr22lliinnee utility recognizes the following options:

     --aa | ----aaddddrreesssseess
             Display the address prior to the line number information.

     --bb _t_a_r_g_e_t | ----ttaarrggeett=_t_a_r_g_e_t
             This option is recognized by aaddddrr22lliinnee but is ignored.  It is
             supported for compatibility with GNU binutils.

     --ee _p_a_t_h_n_a_m_e | ----eexxee=_p_a_t_h_n_a_m_e
             Use the ELF object specified by argument _p_a_t_h_n_a_m_e to translate
             addresses.  If this option is not specified, aaddddrr22lliinnee will use
             the file “_a_._o_u_t”.

     --ff | ----ffuunnccttiioonnss
             Display function names in addition to file and line number infor‐
             mation.

     --ii | ----iinnlliinneess
             If the address specified belongs to an inlined function, also
             display the line number information for its caller, recursively
             until the first non-inlined caller.

     --jj _s_e_c_t_i_o_n_n_a_m_e | ----sseeccttiioonn=_s_e_c_t_i_o_n_n_a_m_e
             The values specified by arguments _h_e_x_a_d_d_r_e_s_s are to be treated as
             offsets into the section named _s_e_c_t_i_o_n_n_a_m_e.

     --pp | ----pprreettttyy--pprriinntt
             Display the line number information on one line, in human read‐
             able manner.

     --ss | ----bbaasseennaammee
             Display only the base name for each file name.

     --CC | ----ddeemmaannggllee
             Demangle C++ names.

     --HH | ----hheellpp
             Print a help message.

     --VV | ----vveerrssiioonn
             Print a version identifier and exit.

OOUUTTPPUUTT FFOORRMMAATT
     If the --ff option was not specified, aaddddrr22lliinnee will print the file name
     and line number for each address specified on a separate line.

     If the --ff option was specified, aaddddrr22lliinnee will print a line containing
     the name of the function corresponding to program address _h_e_x_a_d_d_r_e_s_s,
     followed by a line with the file name and line number.

     If the --pp option was specified, aaddddrr22lliinnee will print line number informa‐
     tion and function name on one line in human readable manner. If the --ii
     option was also specified, aaddddrr22lliinnee will print the caller function in‐
     formation prefixed with “(inlined by)”.

     The aaddddrr22lliinnee utility prints the file name and line number using the for‐
     mat “FILENAME:LINENUMBER”.

     If a file or function name could not be determined, aaddddrr22lliinnee will print
     a question mark in their place.  If the line number could not be deter‐
     mined, aaddddrr22lliinnee will print a zero in its place.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     To map address 080483c4 in the default executable _a_._o_u_t to a source file
     name and line number use:
           % addr2line 080483c4

     To map address 080483c4 in executable _h_e_l_l_o_w_o_r_l_d, use:
           % addr2line -e helloworld 080483c4

     To have aaddddrr22lliinnee act as a filter reading addresses from its standard in‐
     put use:
           % addr2line

     To print the function name corresponding to an address in addition to its
     source file and line number use:
           % addr2line -f 080483c4

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The aaddddrr22lliinnee utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs.

